<clickhouse>
    <!--
      What the `default` user may do: everything EXCEPT reach outside the
      server or stop it.

      Every service (api, api-public, api-data, api-mcp, derivations, the
      indexers, the backups, schema-bootstrap) and operator access provisioning
      connect as `default`. Without a grants list an XML user holds ALL, which
      includes the table functions and engines that read or write outside the database
      (file, url, s3, remote, mysql, postgresql, jdbc, odbc, hdfs, kafka, ...),
      BACKUP TO a disk or bucket, and every SYSTEM command (SHUTDOWN, KILL,
      STOP MERGES, DROP caches). A leaked password or an injected query should
      not turn into file reads, outbound requests or a stopped server.

      Measured before removing them (a week of system.query_log, 33.7M
      queries): used_table_functions empty for every query; no query of kind
      System, Backup or Restore; no BACKUP or SOURCES privilege in
      used_privileges. The backups export client side
      (SELECT ... FORMAT Parquet), the schema has no URL/File/Kafka engines and
      no dictionaries. The only SYSTEM commands anyone runs are by hand:
      SYSTEM RELOAD CONFIG (the users.d reload procedure in AGENTS.md) and
      SYSTEM FLUSH LOGS (before reading query_log), so those two stay.

      Kept on purpose: all DDL and DML on every database, KILL QUERY, OPTIMIZE,
      and access management WITH GRANT OPTION, which access provisioning needs
      for CREATE USER / ROLE / SETTINGS PROFILE / QUOTA and GRANT/REVOKE.

      How this merges with the entrypoint: the official image rewrites
      users.d/default-user.xml on every start with <access_management>1. A
      <grants> list cannot coexist with that flag (the user then fails to load:
      "Any other access control settings can't be specified with grants"), so
      it is removed here and access management comes from GRANT ALL instead.
      This file sorts after default-user.xml, so it is merged on top of it on
      every start. With a <grants> list the access_management flag no longer
      gates NAMED COLLECTION ADMIN, so that is revoked explicitly (the
      access_management flag alone never granted it).

      Sources in 26.x are enforced as READ / WRITE ON <source>; REVOKE SOURCES
      removes those. SHOW GRANTS still lists the legacy global names (FILE, URL,
      S3, ...) after the revoke; they no longer authorize anything: file(),
      url(), s3(), remote(), mysql(), postgresql(), hdfs(), ENGINE = URL and
      INSERT INTO FUNCTION all answer ACCESS_DENIED "READ ON FILE" / "WRITE ON
      REMOTE".

      Reloads without a restart: copy into the container's
      /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/ and run SYSTEM RELOAD CONFIG; verify on a
      NEW connection with SHOW GRANTS FOR default. To undo, delete the file from
      users.d and reload.
    -->
    <users>
        <default>
            <access_management remove="remove"/>
            <grants>
                <query>GRANT ALL ON *.* WITH GRANT OPTION</query>
                <query>REVOKE SOURCES ON *.*</query>
                <query>REVOKE BACKUP ON *.*</query>
                <query>REVOKE NAMED COLLECTION ADMIN ON *</query>
                <query>REVOKE SYSTEM ON *.*</query>
                <query>GRANT SYSTEM RELOAD, SYSTEM FLUSH ON *.* WITH GRANT OPTION</query>
            </grants>
        </default>
    </users>
</clickhouse>
